 thing predictable about  flu is that it's unpredictable  uh but having said that i would predict  that that we're probably not going to  have a bad flu season in this calendar  year  people are staying in too much there's  not that much travel  so it would be quite surprising if we  saw a lot of flu they're not  seeing flu in the southern hemisphere  when they usually do  so it's likely that we're not going to  see flu at least through this year but  by all means get a flu shot  and this is just one more reason to be  careful about coving the things we do  for covid washing our hands wearing a  mask  washing our distance are going to reduce  other infections as well so even if  coveted  isn't around they do some good  fair enough and we are heading into an  election on november 3rd i'm wondering  what your take is on how the president  has handled the pandemic so far  well i think there's not the slightest  doubt that the u.s  has been a laggard that our response has  failed and because of that we have  one of the higher death rates in the  world because of that  we have many more jobs and lives lost  and  could have been lost otherwise there  really is a lot more we can do  to be safer to have an organized  response to have  structured communication about it to  tell people what they can do  to help people address problems to scale  up  a comprehensive approach to have clear  indicators  the list of things that are not being  done well  by the us government really goes on and  on and on and if you if you look at  places as varied as iceland germany  austria denmark taiwan  hong kong singapore rwanda  senegal you're talking about places that  have 5  10 50 100 times lower death rates  than the united states we really could  have done  so much better it didn't have to be this  bad  and we of course are heading into the  debate tonight  is there anything that you are hoping to  hear from either candidate that would  give you  a little bit of peace about how the  country could handle coven 19.  you know i wish we would all recognize  that we are all connected  that infection in  any individual in any group in any part  of the country  is a risk everywhere and we're all in it  together  and the more we recognize that the  better off we'll be  the more anyone divides us  the more the virus can continue to  conquer us  the more we recognize that we're in it  together the more we can  push the virus back and get more of our  lives and our livelihoods back  and we do have a really young audience  here at quick take do you have a  specific message for young people  about their involvement in this uptick  in numbers  you know i'm encouraged despite some  media reports the vast majority of  people young and old  are really concerned about making others  sick and understand  that even if you don't have any symptoms  at all or feel only mildly ill from it  you could end up infecting someone else  and they could die from it none of us  want  that on our conscience none of us want  that to happen and  just keep in mind this will pass it may  feel like forever  but it's not it's going to pass um  it'll be better by late 2021 a year from  now  and within the next few years uh we'll  be to a new normal  things will be different maybe we'll be  used to not going out when we're sick  why should you expose other people when  you're sick stay home  take care of yourself and hopefully  we'll be in a world that recognizes our  common humanity  and is better to one another because of  it you mentioned  2021 uh what is your current thinking  around a timeline for a vaccine  we don't know anyone who says they know  what's going to happen doesn't know  enough  we don't have any proof that a vaccine  works is effective  it's manufactured can be delivered we're  guardedly optimistic there will be  vaccines  but a vaccine is not going to bring a  fairy tale ending  to this pandemic it's going to be one  more tool in our toolbox  and we have to keep all of 